But we can't call bb.get(i) after we have consumed the buffer earlier? The method consumes bytes via getLong() and get() etc., which advances position. At the end, bb's position will be at limit (n). So we cannot read bytes after that. However spec may just refer to original content; but we cannot revert. In spec we can use \old(bb) to capture original state. Use \old(bb) to refer to original ByteBuffer before modifications. However we cannot call methods on ByteBuffer that are not observer? The rule says only publicly accessible observer methods provided in the test function. Here we are inside the same class; we can refer to bb parameter. Using \old(bb) is allowed? Usually \old(expression) captures old value of expression before method execution. So we can use \old(bb) to refer to original buffer. But we also need to capture its original position? In spec we can use \old(bb) but not its internal state? In JML, you can refer to \old(bb) which is the same object but its state before method call. So we can use \old(bb.get(i))? However get modifies position, so not pure. Not allowed as observer. Maybe we can use bb.duplicate()? Not allowed.

Simplify: assert \result != null && (\forall int i; 0 <= i && i < \result.toByteArray().length; \result.toByteArray()[i] == \old(bb.get(i)));

